{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/332484-to-the-guys-who-have-played-wraith-lets-teach-others-how-to-play-wraith","id":332484,"title":"To the guys who have played Wraith: Lets teach others how to play Wraith!","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-06T14:04:02-08:00","id":332484,"title":"To the guys who have played Wraith: Lets teach others how to play Wraith!","content":"One of the first things most cWoD gamers will say when the topic of Wraith comes up is &quot;Yeah, it's a cool idea but it's unplayable.&quot;<br /> <br /> Now, I know that's not true. I have played and enjoyed Wraith on many occasions and had buckets of fun. But still, it's the impression many people have of this beautiful game.<br /> <br /> So, now that Wr20 is a reality and Wraith will have it's rennaissance, I feel the time has come for those of us in the community to show everyone who is even slightly interested in Wraith that the game is not only playable, it's brilliant. I know many people will back and/or buy the book for completions sake, having invested in all the other books as well, and leave it on their shelf to gather dust and moan softly in the night, when they could have actually played the game and had some of the best roleplaying experiences of their lives!<br /> <br /> So let's do this!<br /> <br /> To the people who have gotten the impression that the game is too difficult to play, to demanding or even downright unplayable, come here and ask your questions. I (and hopefully others who have played and loved Wraith) will answer to the best of my ability.<br /> <br /> To the guys out there who have played and enjoyed Wraith, join me! Let's tell people just how great an experience it can be!<br /> <br /> First of all, I feel I need to adress the Shadowguide. I have seen a lot of people call the system broken, unusable and unplayable. Two players per character? How could that possibly work?<br /> <br /> In my experience, it creates awesome roleplaying opportunities. I think some players get stuck in a competitive mindset when it comes to Shadowguiding. They read the rules and think &quot;ok, so I'm supposed to work against this guy and be an asshole to him. How is that fun?&quot; It doesn't have to be a competition though, and ideally it never should. Shadowguiding isn't about messing up a player character, it's about helping the player to tell awesome stories about his character! You get to be a part of a two man team whose job it is to put focus on the issues that really matter to that character. The mechanics will help you do this. Passions and Dark Passions, Fetters and Thorns, all these things create a vortex of opportunity to tell amazing, deep and passionate stories about a ghost with strong and emotional ties to the world of the living.<br /> The key part is to go into it with these expectations in mind. Being a Shadowguide is not about being an asshole, it's about helping the player bring out the best and worst in his character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4449,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4449-eisenkreutzer","user":"EisenKreutzer","date":". 12-06-2014, 06:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4449-eisenkreutzer","name":"EisenKreutzer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4449_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T15:16:16-08:00","id":332517,"title":"","content":"Be mentally ill. Use WtO as free therapy. There is no step 3.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T15:46:18-08:00","id":332523,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/332484-to-the-guys-who-have-played-wraith-lets-teach-others-how-to-play-wraith?p=332517#post332517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Be mentally ill. Use WtO as free therapy. There is no step 3.</div> </div> </div> </div>...somehow I feel I should be less offended by this than I am.As it stands, I'm going to call it 'not funny'.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T15:46:57-08:00","id":332524,"title":"","content":"I have a sudden need for an unlike button.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T17:42:38-08:00","id":332609,"title":"","content":"Let me clarify; I'm severely depressed (among other things, like pretty gnarly insomnia and anxiety) and have been for several years. I've been everywhere from therapists' offices to psych wards. To call it brutal would be something of an understatement. <br /> <br /> Wraith got me through a lot of this shit. The ever-present draw of Oblivion, the option to just let go and be turned into nothing... that's not exactly fiction to me. You need to care about things to stay in one piece, your Fetters and Passions that keep your head above water. Your Shadow, that jackass who tells you how things will go wrong, how much easier it would be to give up? He's damn real, and hating him only gives him more fuel because he is you. You have to make peace with it in order to move on. <br /> <br /> Wraith is less a game to me and more a pretty beautiful example of interactive art, the best way to explain my struggle to a few of my friends, and a damn clever little bundle of world-building. I meant no offense and apologize if I caused any.<br /> <br /> <br /> ...and I cannot wait to get my Silent Legion tattoo.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":281,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","user":"atamajakki","date":". 12-06-2014, 08:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T18:19:17-08:00","id":332634,"title":"","content":"But enough about me! I think I owe this thread some on-topic stuff.<br /> <br /> To me, Wraith works best as a psychodrama, and that means you need tragic protagonists. I like to build from the flaws on up for my characters. I had a player come up with a gal who lived in an abusive relationship, and never had the strength to get out of it before she died. Her disgust with who she was meant quickly found a love for Moliate (in a desperate attempt to change herself externally, in the hopes of fixing the person inside) and Castigate (so as to help others make sure they never end up as weak as she was). Most of her Dark Passions were fixated on her still-living abuser (a confusing jumble of wanting to hurt him back and to try and redeem him) while her Passions revolved around the concept of moving on from him and finding new love. That's a damn good character right there, built from &quot;I wasn't strong enough.&quot;<br /> <br /> There's a funny expression I've heard a few times that I really like: &quot;Find someone whose inner demons get along with your own.&quot; I take that as gospel for Wraith troupes, and encourage folks to make characters together. Don't go with anything so blatant as &quot;we were friends/co-workers/whatever&quot; in real-life (the Underworld is far too lonely for such happy accidents), but make your flaws work together. Say you have a father who helps other people with a self-destructive lack of regard for his own well-being and a cheerleader who starved herself in a desperate attempt to earn validation from someone, anyone; those two have the makings of an unhealthy bond that will keep them together (and cause lots of drama!) even if the plot loses steam. You don't just need unhealthy codependency; people with similar experiences and traumas are likely to band together just because having someone else who understands is a rare blessing.<br /> <br /> As for running the game itself, I always advocate for running fast and loose with the rules, and tailoring your experience to the players' wishes. My idea of Wraith doesn't appeal to many, but my group was more than happy to have some fun with it as &quot;horror game with the added novelty of already being dead.&quot; We've also tossed around &quot;the Underworld as a means to explore all the lost civilizations of history,&quot; which is awesome and has little angst at all! Find something that works for everyone, and always remember that your end goal is fun.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-07T02:43:14-08:00","id":332877,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/332484-to-the-guys-who-have-played-wraith-lets-teach-others-how-to-play-wraith?p=332609#post332609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let me clarify; I'm severely depressed (among other things, like pretty gnarly insomnia and anxiety) and have been for several years. I've been everywhere from therapists' offices to psych wards. To call it brutal would be something of an understatement.<br /> <br /> Wraith got me through a lot of this shit. The ever-present draw of Oblivion, the option to just let go and be turned into nothing... that's not exactly fiction to me. You need to care about things to stay in one piece, your Fetters and Passions that keep your head above water. Your Shadow, that jackass who tells you how things will go wrong, how much easier it would be to give up? He's damn real, and hating him only gives him more fuel because he is you. You have to make peace with it in order to move on.<br /> <br /> Wraith is less a game to me and more a pretty beautiful example of interactive art, the best way to explain my struggle to a few of my friends, and a damn clever little bundle of world-building. I meant no offense and apologize if I caused any.<br /> <br /> <br /> ...and I cannot wait to get my Silent Legion tattoo.</div> </div> </div> </div>I retract my earlier statement, then. At first it kind of sounded like you were saying that in order to enjoy wraith you would have to be mentally ill...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-07T08:43:58-08:00","id":333007,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/332484-to-the-guys-who-have-played-wraith-lets-teach-others-how-to-play-wraith?p=332634#post332634\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But enough about me! I think I owe this thread some on-topic stuff.<br /> <br /> To me, Wraith works best as a psychodrama, and that means you need tragic protagonists. I like to build from the flaws on up for my characters. I had a player come up with a gal who lived in an abusive relationship, and never had the strength to get out of it before she died. Her disgust with who she was meant quickly found a love for Moliate (in a desperate attempt to change herself externally, in the hopes of fixing the person inside) and Castigate (so as to help others make sure they never end up as weak as she was). Most of her Dark Passions were fixated on her still-living abuser (a confusing jumble of wanting to hurt him back and to try and redeem him) while her Passions revolved around the concept of moving on from him and finding new love. That's a damn good character right there, built from &quot;I wasn't strong enough.&quot;<br /> <br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>Similarly, in one of the Wraith games I ran, one of the characters was transgendered. She (identified as fully female) also immediately (and obviously) gravitated towards Moliate. In some ways, her death allowed her to fulfill her lifetime desires, in a sort of post hoc way. Her Shadows was the &quot;Be a Man&quot; identification she had struggled against her entire life. The dance between the two was very compelling (especially since, as stated, Moliate was front and center on the table). There was also an Artifact in play that allowed interesting juxtapositions of self-identity. (I put that in before the characters were made. It was synchronicity that the story of the Artifact and the story of the character were dramatically nearly perfect for each other). The character could have been an offensive disaster of stereotyping and misunderstanding, but it was handled very well by both the player and the Shadowguide. Probably the best two at those interactions at the table. <br /> <br /> TBH, (and to get to the topic), I don't know of many other games that could have even begun to handle that storyline as well as Wraith. The setting and rules really enabled the kind of exploration of those ideas that just wouldn't have been as fully realized elsewhere. I guess we could tackle the same issues in a Mage game, but it would have come across very differently. Probably something more along a mystic union of opposites, a creation of a rebis entity.... Very cool but not really intrinsically as personal or visceral.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-17T09:51:09-08:00","id":343057,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>EisenKreutzer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/332484-to-the-guys-who-have-played-wraith-lets-teach-others-how-to-play-wraith#post332484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To the guys out there who have played and enjoyed Wraith, join me! Let's tell people just how great an experience it can be!</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the way to introduce the game to someone is to talk them into trying it. Toward that end:<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/343041-tips-for-running-demo-convention-games\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/343041-tips-for-running-demo-convention-games\"> tips for running demo/convention games - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/343041-tips-for-running-demo-convention-games\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/343041-tips-for-running-demo-convention-games </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> What wisdom does everyone have regarding exhibition one-shots of Wraith? I've run maybe three over the years, and have now been inspired to do another one soon. I'd appreciate any tips people have. With any RPG, there are differences in how you'd set up a campaign vs. a one-shot with pregenerated </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4448-bphillips","name":"bphillips","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4448_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-21T10:19:49-08:00","id":346796,"title":"","content":"Okey. I have never played Wraith but have, since I found the Wr20 kickstarter, read 2nd ed pdf.<br /> <br /> The tricky part for me to get my head around is how to balance the shadow i.e. how not to make the shadow into a something tat just &quot;messes up&quot; a character for another player. But rather a more multidimensional antagonist. How have you more experienced Wraith players handled their shadowguiding?<br /> <br /> My understanding how the shadow would be something like this. Say I have a character who is a complete saint (caregiver nature) and works with his castigate and argos to provide glimmer of light for other spirits. The shadow would then be a brute out to do the exact opposite, a bully out to hurt others and cause pain. Now with shadowguiding the player would be forced to alternate between his saint and bully characters because of catharsis. In fact if you create any character you want to play, there always be part of your playtime were you play that character polar opposite. Is this a correct assumption?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4574-laughing-lord","name":"Laughing Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-21T11:35:26-08:00","id":346861,"title":"","content":"Why is this character going into catharsis all the time? What is she/he doing to wrack up so much Angst? Catharsis doesn't happen all that often unless the character is somehow wracking up Angst. Considering how far apart the Shadow and the Psyche are with this character, what is this brutal nasty hateful Shadow offering that is so enticing to this nurturing, loving, beacon of hope Psyche? Catharsis and Harrowing are important to the game but they aren't something that happens every session by any means. And that's not &quot;every session for this one character&quot; that's &quot;not every session for the entire circle of wraiths.&quot;<br /> <br /> Also, the Shadows doesn't have to be such a complete polar opposite. In fact, it doesn't have to have anything to do with how the Psyche's take on the afterlife. They just both have to have something to do with the person who they both were before the death and the Shadow has to have a more nihilistic bent than that Psyche. (Probably. There are always exceptions. A Psyche could be a right bastard while the Shadow might be a great drinking buddy, but that's more a special snowflake situation.) This character could just as easily have a smarmy salesman Shadow (Soooo... We healed him, now, what's he gonna do for us?). The Shadow could be terrified child (No! No! No! Don't risk us by going into the Tempest just to HELP him! The Tempest is scaaaarey! And I'm sure someone else can help him, it doesn't HAVE to be you...). The Shadow could be psychoanalyzing the Psyche all the time. (Now we both know why we're going to help him and it doesn't really have anything to do with you being altruistic, does it? It's all about the fact that you couldn't save you little brother from his heroin addiction... When are you going to stop wasting time trying to help people who don't realy need your help and deal with the REAL issues....). It could be an &quot;I told you so&quot; type that waits until after the good deed to pounce on some negative outcome and grind a sense of failure into the Psyche. Etc. Etc.<br /> <br /> This also seems to assume that the Shadowguide is going to be acting in a very hostile manner against the Psyche's player. Not in a &quot;we're all here to play a game to have fun&quot; way, but in a &quot;ha! ha! ha! I now have the power to make your character suck and make sure you have a miserable time during the session! Because that's how I get my jollies!&quot; I know players of the second ilk are out there, but if that's your group.... you might want to think of playing something other than Wraith. Honestly, if that's even ONE person in your group, you might want to wait on it. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-21T12:44:56-08:00","id":346890,"title":"","content":"Thanks for a great reply Ajax! Your examples is exactly how I would want to play the shadow. That is spot on what I am hoping to get across in a game. It just seemed more confrontational/hostile when I read the rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4574-laughing-lord","name":"Laughing Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-21T13:28:33-08:00","id":346935,"title":"","content":"Ajax hit it right on the nose and about the only I can add to that is to pick up the <i>Shadow Player's Guide</i>. It's a definite must get for any WtO players and Storytellers as it expands on the material in the Rulebook and is very helpful in getting a handle on Shadowguiding. Seriously, if you really want to get into Wraith the <i>Shadow Player's Guide </i>is one of the must get books.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-21T15:54:41-08:00","id":347045,"title":"","content":"One thing I cannot stress enough: the Shadow is not your evil twin. It is not your exact opposite. The Shadow is the part of yourself you hate, the things you don't admit to having. Your Shadow is a self-destructive pile of repression and shame, every impulse and cruel thought you work so hard to keep down. Catharsis doesn't have to be an exercise in mustache-twirling, puppy-kicking villainy.<br /> <br /> But these things work better as examples. Say a Wraith has Protect My Wife (Love) as one of his primary Passions, looking out for his darling across the veil. A case could easily be made for Keep My Wife Loyal (Greed) as a Dark Passion; though he'd never admit it, our hypothetical fellow would hate for his wife to move on from him and ever love again. Let's say he spots his darling going on a date with a nice young man; his Shadow whispers to him about how this guy looks dangerous, he uses some fun Arcanoi to write a bloody message in the mirror when her date goes to the bathroom, and he scares away the suitor while doing what he thinks fulfills one of his Passions. The Shadow is insidious like that; he makes you do what you don't like to admit you want, with self-destruction as the eventual goal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":281,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","user":"atamajakki","date":". 12-21-2014, 07:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-21T22:14:34-08:00","id":347163,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Laughing Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/332484-to-the-guys-who-have-played-wraith-lets-teach-others-how-to-play-wraith?p=346796#post346796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> My understanding how the shadow would be something like this. Say I have a character who is a complete saint (caregiver nature) and works with his castigate and argos to provide glimmer of light for other spirits. The shadow would then be a brute out to do the exact opposite, a bully out to hurt others and cause pain.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, that kind of psyche/shadow pairing is somewhat unlikely. What you should be looking for is not the opposite of a given emotion, but the dark side of that same emotion. For instance, the Psyche may believe that he helps people because of Altruism, Compassion, or Penitence...and he is right, partially. However for the Shadow, this may be a matter of pride (Seek Praise from others for Being Such a Swell Guy), manipulation (Help Others So They Feel Obligated to Me), or denial (Redirect Blame By Faking a Nice Guy Persona). The psyche would never admit he does good things purely to satisfy his own ego, and thus would shove these things into the province of the Shadow.<br /> <br /> A good Psyche for a brutish Shadow is likely a character who does use violence, but normally would use it towaards a &quot;higher cause&quot;: a soldier in service to patriotism, a policeman in service to justice, or a gang member in service to loyalty. They won't want to admit to themselves that they also engage in violence because of rage, racism, despair, or violence for it's own sake, and thus repress it to their Shadows.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-08T11:08:24-08:00","id":363688,"title":"","content":"So, I am going to be running a Wraith One-Shot soon (And if it goes well a Chronicle) for about six players who will all be new to the game.<br /> As such are there any tips for getting players quickly into it?<br /> Only two of us will actually have the Wraith Book.<br /> <br /> Also, I got a little confusion on when a Wraith needs to spend corpus in accordance with the rule of ouch.<br /> But what about things that do not cause ouch? Would a slow moving car make a Wraith spend Corpus?<br /> What about if in a bustling crowd? Will the folks just pass through the wraith without them having to spend Corpus?<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/1n0ov7p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3881-mental-mouse","name":"Mental-Mouse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3881_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"124"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-10T23:05:39-08:00","id":366086,"title":"","content":"Would it hurt if a car slowly ran over you? Yeah. So that's Corpus.<br /> <br /> Skinlands objects DO exert physical force on Wraiths - you can lean on a building and not have to go through it, for example. So a Wraith can walk through a bustling crowd so long as they have somewhere in said crowd to squeeze through and and they don't mind getting pushed around. Likewise a slow moving car might push them out of the way rather than flat go through them. Corpus loss is mostly supposed to be defensive - you take a point of Corpus when you would likely take much more serious damage normally. There are of course edge cases, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-11T06:09:43-08:00","id":366226,"title":"","content":"As rules are always subjective, I allow myself to introduce a little nuance.<br /> <br /> It may come from my experience with Orpheus, but for me a Wraith in the Shadowlands is stuck by everything with the same strengh, may it be a drying sheet or a wall in steel. Even the pages of an open book are as solid as rock for a wraith, the only thing they can do (apart from Arcanoi of course) is to discorporate themselves for going through. And it always hurts.<br /> <br /> The two main factors for deciding when the players have to spend Corpus are imo the '<b>speed</b>' of the collision (of either the wraith if they are running through a wall, or of the object in case of a thrown baseball for instance) and also the '<b>mass</b>' of the collision, meaning both the thickness of the object the wraith is going through and how big is the part of the wraith going through. And I totally agree with the fact that moving things might rather push the wraith away, especially if they are slow.<br /> <br /> After that, I think it's a matter of personal scale. Personally, my players wouldn't spend Corpus in order to walk entirely through a wall if they have time to do so, but they would have to do so if they are for instance running away from barghests. Would the wall be 2 metters thick, they would have to spend a Corpus in any case. Following the same logic, I would ask them to spend a (single) Corpus for going through a bustling crowd.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4701-dam","name":"Dam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4701_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-13T19:47:57-08:00","id":369327,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Laughing Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/332484-to-the-guys-who-have-played-wraith-lets-teach-others-how-to-play-wraith?p=346796#post346796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">. . . My understanding how the shadow would be something like this. Say I have a character who is a complete saint (caregiver nature) and works with his castigate and argos to provide glimmer of light for other spirits. The shadow would then be a brute out to do the exact opposite, a bully out to hurt others and cause pain. Now with shadowguiding the player would be forced to alternate between his saint and bully characters because of catharsis. In fact if you create any character you want to play, there always be part of your playtime were you play that character polar opposite. Is this a correct assumption?</div> </div> </div> </div>As the others have mentioned, a wraith's Shadow isn't his direct opposite. Its a complimentary/contrasting personality, built out of the wraith's repressed urges and desires. So your example wraith/shadow pairing is unlikely. <br /> <br /> But you could get close to something like that:<br /> <br /> * In life, Melvin was always meek and quiet. Raised by his grandmother, who strongly believed that children should be seen and not heard, he internalized the idea that he should always be a &quot;good boy&quot;, and &quot;good boys never got angry&quot;. People who knew him were impressed with his old-school, impeccable manners. And no one ever remembers seeing him get angry. <br /> <br /> In death, all the anger that he never let himself feel in life comes out in his shadow. In catharsis (i.e. when his shadow is dominant), Melvin screams at crying babies to shut up, flips off old ladies as they cross the road . . . His fellow wraiths still speak in awe of the time he grabbed a high-ranking Hierarch by his relic tie, and cursed him out in terms so vulgar and profane, his rant literally peeled the paint off the wall. Or the time he stormed into a Renegade haunt, and beat five of the ugliest thugs they had ever seen into Harrowings (i.e. unconscious), just because he &quot;needed to hit something&quot;.<br /> <br /> * Before her heart attack, Dr. Carmen Rivera was a brilliant heart surgeon, saving countless lives. But as the years went on, and surgery piled up on surgery, she began to wonder. Just what would it feel like, to reach out and crush a patient's heart? It's right there, right in front of her . . . lub dub, lub dub . . . .after all the lives she's saved, surely it'd be okay if she did it - just once? <br /> <br /> Of course, in life, she'd always just shake her head to clear away that thought . . . as a wraith, though, her shadow is devoted to experiencing all the things she didn't have time for in life. A one night stand with a cute guy who's name she never learned. Blowing off work. And exploring certain medical . . . &quot;experiments&quot;. She doesn't yet have the arcanoi to help (or hurt) with a surgery, but her shadow <i>did</i> skin an enemy &quot;alive&quot; with a relic scalpel, just to see what it felt like. And being dead, she has all the time in the world for that &quot;heart thing&quot;. . .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T14:40:13-08:00","id":370144,"title":"","content":"I've previously described an anxiety disorder as a survival instinct with no sense of context. That's a pretty good way to get a handle on how to run a Shadow beyond just &quot;I do something devilishly <i>eeevil!!!</i>&quot;<br /> <br /> As an aside, Better Angels is a supervillain game by the fantastic Greg Stolze that borrows the Shadowguiding mechanic (they give proper thanks in a sidebar within the book) for the demon inside your head that gives you powers. These demons are explicitly &quot;eeevil&quot; and serve as nice contrast to the nuance required of proper Shadowguiding.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":281,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","user":"atamajakki","date":". 01-14-2015, 05:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}